// ---- Recycle plan: the tear CUT + the dominant eigenvalue method ----
//
//  This case is process05_isomerization_recycle with ONE change: the recycle
//  is closed by `recycleSolver DominantEigenvalue;`, the dominant eigenvalue
//  method of O. Orbach and C. M. Crowe, Can. J. Chem. Eng. 49 (1971) 509
//  (formulas, safeguards and what is Choupo's own addition are listed in
//  src/solver/DominantEigenvalue.H).  Near the solution the differences of
//  successive direct-substitution iterates shrink by a factor lambda per
//  pass (the paper's Eq 15-17).  The aid estimates lambda from the norm
//  ratio of two consecutive differences, waits until two successive
//  estimates agree, and then MOVES to the apparent solution
//  X_n-1 + alpha (X_n - X_n-1)/(1 - lambda)   (the paper's Eq 18).
//  It REPORTS ALOUD: the numbers below are announced (defaults marked), each
//  move prints its lambda, and a refused move falls back to direct
//  substitution and prints why.
//
//  The damping alpha is declared 1.0 HERE.  The paper's value, 0.7, is the
//  default: it "suppresses oscillation" and costs speed where there is none.
//  On this loop lambda = 0.21 is small and positive, nothing oscillates, and
//  the undamped move lands on the solution.
//
//  MEASURED on this flowsheet, iterations to |dtear|2 < 1e-5:
//      Newton on tears            2
//      Wegstein (q in [-1,0])     9
//      direct substitution        10   (Wegstein with q = 0)
//      DominantEigenvalue a=1.0   6    (the case as declared)
//      DominantEigenvalue a=0.7   10   (the paper's damping: no gain here)
//  HONEST LIMITS, measured the same way on the corpus: on a loop with a
//  small lambda there is little to promote, and the method is built for
//  lambda near 1 (the paper's loops have lambda 0.9 to 0.98).  Where this
//  corpus's loops converge in a handful of passes, DominantEigenvalue
//  matches or trails Wegstein (membrane27: 15 against 7; acetonePlant: 6
//  against 4), and cavett01_recycle_train, which defeats Wegstein, defeats it
//  too.  It is an opt-in aid for slow geometric loops, not a better default.
tearStreams           ( recycle );
recycleSolver          DominantEigenvalue;
recycleTol             1e-5;
recycleMaxIter         150;
recycleDEDamping       1.0;     // the paper's Eq 18 alpha; its default is 0.7
// Left at their defaults (and announced as such):
// recycleDELambdaTol     0.01;   // two successive lambda within 1 % (paper: 0.5-1 %)
// recycleDELambdaMax     0.9;    // refuse a move when |lambda| is above this (Choupo's own bound)
// recycleDEMinCosine     0.9;    // refuse a move when the differences are not parallel (Choupo's own guard)
